# Outbox Pattern

Source of truth: `docs/architecture/infrastructure.md`.

## Why

Persist domain change + outbox row in the **same** `UnitOfWork` transaction, then relay to BullMQ so jobs are not lost on crash.

```
use case (UoW)
  → save aggregates + insert outbox row
  → commit
outbox relay (apps/worker)
  → publish to BullMQ
  → mark processed
processor (apps/worker)
  → e.g. send invite email, write audit
```

## What Goes Through Outbox

✅ Email / notifications, audit fan-out, cross-context reliable reactions  
❌ Synchronous invariants, real-time UI (use EventBus / pub-sub)

## Example Events

- `MemberInvited` → notifications queue (invite email)
- `TenantCreated` → audit + welcome notification
- `RoleAssigned` → invalidate effective-permissions cache + audit

## Outbox Row (persistence model)

```typescript
@Entity('outbox')
export class OutboxEntryEntity {
  @PrimaryColumn('uuid') id!: string
  @Column() eventType!: string // e.g. 'MemberInvited'
  @Column({ type: 'jsonb' }) payload!: Record<string, unknown>
  @Column() status!: 'pending' | 'processed' | 'failed'
  @Column({ type: 'uuid', nullable: true }) tenantId?: string
  @Column({ type: 'timestamptz' }) createdAt!: Date
}
```

Always include `tenantId` in the payload when the work is tenant-scoped so the worker can re-establish `TenantContext`.

## Worker Must Restore Tenant Context

```typescript
@Processor('notifications')
export class NotificationsProcessor extends WorkerHost {
  async process(job: Job<{ tenantId: string; email: string }>) {
    return TenantContext.run({ tenantId: job.data.tenantId }, async () => {
      await this.sendInviteEmail.execute(job.data)
    })
  }
}
```

## Placement

- Outbox write: inside application use case / handler within UoW
- Relay + BullMQ adapters: `infrastructure-messaging`
- Processors: `apps/worker` (thin) calling application use cases
